<head>
  <title>Limitations — Azox</title>
  <meta name="description" content="What Azox does not do yet, stated plainly, so you can judge whether it fits." />
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css" />
  <script src="/site.js" defer></script>
</head>

<script>
  import Nav from '../../components/Nav.azox';
  import Footer from '../../components/Footer.azox';
  import DocsNav from '../../components/DocsNav.azox';
  import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.azox';
</script>

<div class="shell">
  <Nav version="0.1.0" current="docs" />

  <div class="container docs">
    <DocsNav current="/docs/limitations" />

    <main class="doc-body">
      <h1>Limitations</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Azox is at v0.1.0. This page lists what it does not do, so you can
        decide whether it fits your project rather than finding out halfway
        through building one.
      </p>

      <h2>No lifecycle hooks</h2>
      <p>
        Components <a href="/docs/components">can hold state</a>, but there is
        nothing that runs when one appears or goes away — no mount, unmount or
        cleanup hook. A component's script runs once, when the component is
        built.
      </p>
      <p>
        This matters if you need to start a timer, open a subscription, or
        attach a listener to <code>window</code> and take it down again later.
      </p>

      <h2>Lists rebuild rather than reconcile</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/docs/control-flow"><code>&lt;each&gt;</code> and
        <code>&lt;if&gt;</code></a> exist, but there is no keyed
        reconciliation: when a list's source changes, the block's contents are
        rebuilt rather than matched against what was already there.
      </p>
      <p>
        For most lists this is not noticeable. It matters for a list that
        updates very often, or one holding focus or scroll position inside it,
        since those are lost on a rebuild. Bindings outside the list keep
        their fine-grained behaviour.
      </p>

      <h2>No dynamic routes</h2>
      <p>
        Every route is a file that exists at build time. There is no
        <code>[slug].azox</code>, and no way to generate pages from data.
      </p>

      <h2>No client-side routing</h2>
      <p>
        Links are ordinary page loads. There is no client router, no prefetching
        and no view transitions. Each page does arrive as server-rendered HTML.
      </p>

      <h2>Hydration replaces the markup</h2>
      <p>
        The client module clears the server-rendered root and rebuilds it,
        rather than adopting the existing nodes. Content is present before
        JavaScript runs, which is what matters for search engines and slow
        connections, but this is not true hydration and there is a brief
        rebuild on load.
      </p>

      <h2>No shared layout</h2>
      <p>
        Every page repeats its own <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> block, and
        components cannot contribute to the document head. A navigation
        component can be shared; a stylesheet link cannot.
      </p>

      <h2>No CSS pipeline</h2>
      <p>
        Files in <code>public/</code> are copied as-is. Nothing is bundled,
        minified, scoped or hashed. There is no scoped-styles feature: a
        component's styles are global like any other CSS.
      </p>

      <h2>Expressions are evaluated during the build</h2>
      <p>
        Server rendering runs a page's <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block
        through <code>new Function</code>. That is fine for project source you
        wrote, which is the same assumption any template engine's server step
        makes — but never point a build at <code>.azox</code> content submitted
        by someone else.
      </p>

      <h2>Small syntax gaps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A page needs exactly one root element; there are no fragments</li>
        <li>Attribute expressions cannot contain a raw <code>&gt;</code> outside a string</li>
        <li>There is no <code>bind:</code> shorthand for two-way inputs</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What does work</h2>
      <p>
        The parts that exist are tested and behave predictably: signals,
        effects and computed values; components with props and slots; slots
        containing further components; file-based routing at any depth; server
        rendering with escaping; the dev server with live reload; and a build
        that runs on any static host.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <p>
          Every one of these gaps is a deliberate stopping point rather than an
          oversight. Follow
          <a href="https://github.com/darilpratomo/azox">the repository</a> to
          see what lands next.
        </p>
      </div>
    </main>
  </div>

  <Footer />
</div>
